+# ADR 0119: Tangent-Space GPU Contract and Shared Material Fragment Stage
+
+Status: Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+The generic mesh IR and runtime `Mesh` already retain optional source tangents
+as `xyz` plus bitangent handedness in `w`, but the GPU renderer does not consume
+them. Both static and skinned material shaders instead reconstruct a tangent
+basis from screen-space derivatives in every fragment. That fallback is useful
+for formats that do not provide tangents, but it discards higher-quality source
+tangent frames from glTF and FBX and makes mirrored UV handedness dependent on
+the derivative reconstruction.
+
+The static and skinned WGSL modules also each contain their own complete
+StandardLit, ToonLit, shadow, normal-map, and emissive fragment implementation.
+Adding richer PBR inputs or IBL to both copies would make surface behavior drift
+between static and animated versions of the same asset.
+
+ADR 0118 establishes the renderer color-space and texture-semantics contract.
+The next material features need a stable tangent-space input and one compiled
+surface fragment implementation before new texture slots are added.
+
+## Decision
+
+### Tangent stream
+
+`Mesh::tangents` remains optional CPU-side source data and remains separate from
+`Vertex`. The material GPU path adds a dedicated tangent vertex stream using
+shader location 15:
+
+- `xyz` is the object-space tangent direction;
+- `w` is the bitangent handedness supplied by the source;
+- the stream is bound at vertex slot 2 for static material draws and slot 3 for
+  skinned material draws.
+
+Every uploaded `GpuMesh` owns a tangent buffer. When source tangents are absent,
+the buffer contains zero vectors with `w = 0`. That value is an explicit
+"tangent unavailable" marker rather than a fabricated tangent frame.
+
+Keeping the stream separate preserves the existing public `Vertex` layout,
+morph-position upload contract, shadow/outline vertex layouts, and imported
+mesh representation.
+
+### Normal-map basis selection
+
+The material fragment stage consumes the source tangent when `w` is non-zero.
+It orthogonalizes the tangent against the interpolated geometric normal,
+constructs the bitangent from `cross(normal, tangent) * handedness`, and applies
+the sampled tangent-space normal through that TBN basis.
+
+The vertex stage flips handedness when the effective model/deformation matrix
+has negative orientation. This keeps mirrored object transforms consistent with
+the source tangent convention.
+
+When no source tangent is available, the existing derivative-based TBN
+reconstruction remains the fallback. Degenerate source tangents also fall back
+rather than producing NaNs. PMX, built-in geometry, and other current sources
+that do not provide tangents therefore keep their previous normal-map behavior.
+
+A later phase may generate MikkTSpace-compatible tangents for meshes that lack
+source data. This ADR deliberately does not invent a partial or format-specific
+tangent generator merely to remove the derivative fallback.
+
+### One compiled material fragment stage
+
+The static mesh shader module owns the authoritative material `fs_main` and its
+StandardLit/ToonLit/shadow/normal-map helpers. Static pipelines use that module
+for both vertex and fragment stages.
+
+Skinned material pipelines use `mesh_skinned.wgsl::vs_main` only for skeletal
+vertex deformation and pair it with the same `mesh.wgsl::fs_main` fragment
+module used by static meshes. Vertex/fragment linkage is the generic location
+contract, including the tangent frame at location 8.
+
+This makes future material, PBR, IBL, shadow-response, and ToonLit work a single
+compiled fragment implementation. Skinning remains a vertex-deformation
+responsibility rather than a second surface-shading implementation.
+
+The legacy fragment functions still present in `mesh_skinned.wgsl` are not
+selected by any material pipeline and are no longer authoritative. They may be
+removed in a later source-layout cleanup once WGSL source composition is
+restructured without changing runtime behavior.
+
+### Pass boundaries
+
+Shadow and Outline remain independent passes and do not bind the tangent stream.
+Only material pipelines require it. No MMD, PMX, glTF, or FBX-specific render
+path is introduced.
+
+## Compatibility
+
+- No serialized material schema changes.
+- No Stable ID changes.
+- No public `Vertex` layout change.
+- Existing meshes without tangents continue using derivative TBN reconstruction.
+- glTF/FBX meshes that already retain source tangents begin using those tangents
+  in material normal mapping.
+- PMX behavior remains generic and unchanged when no source tangents exist.
+- Static and skinned meshes use the same compiled material fragment stage.
+
+## Rejected alternatives
+
+### Add tangent directly to `Vertex`
+
+Rejected for this phase. `Vertex` is used by built-in meshes, import conversion,
+morph-position uploads, outline passes, and public runtime APIs. A separate
+stream adds the needed GPU contract without coupling unrelated passes to a
+layout expansion.
+
+### Keep derivative TBN for every mesh
+
+Rejected. It throws away already-retained source tangent frames and prevents the
+renderer from honoring tangent-space conventions authored by source tools.
+
+### Add format-specific tangent handling in glTF, FBX, or PMX render paths
+
+Rejected. Tangent-space interpretation is a renderer mesh/material contract.
+Format parsers only normalize and retain source data.
+
+### Continue compiling separate static and skinned material fragments
+
+Rejected. Richer material and lighting work would need to be implemented twice
+and could produce visible drift between static and animated instances of the
+same material.
+
+## Verification
+
+- Unit tests verify the tangent vertex layout and the explicit missing-tangent
+  sentinel.
+- Existing mesh validation continues to reject tangent-count mismatches.
+- Existing importer tests continue to verify retained source tangents.
+- The renderer's GPU material-pipeline matrix validation must accept static and
+  skinned pipelines with their new tangent stream and cross-module fragment
+  linkage.
+
+## Follow-up
+
+The next renderer-foundation step should add the generic Metallic/Roughness and
+Occlusion/AO material texture contract plus normal scale/occlusion strength,
+then add MikkTSpace-compatible tangent generation for assets that require normal
+mapping but do not provide source tangents. Those inputs should target this
+single material fragment stage before IBL is introduced.